# Runtime-owner thread-local allow-list: the functions of the optimised owner
# object (stage2/runtime_owner_c.c, stage0/runtime_llvm.c) allowed to keep a
# thread-local address across a call that can switch context.
#
# The owner gate (`tools/tls-hoist-gate.sh --owner`) fails on any other
# function that materialises a thread-local and can switch, on any function
# that hands a thread-local address to its caller, on a read of the
# scheduler's thread-local outside kai_worker_here, and on an entry here that
# no longer needs to be listed.
#
# Format: `<function> pinned`, one per line. `#` starts a comment.
#
# pinned  The function only ever runs on one thread, so an address it kept
#         across a switch still names that thread's slot. Not verified by the
#         gate: the reason is stated next to each entry.

# The test, bench and property-check harness. A block's body runs on the
# entry fiber, which is pinned to thread 0, and so does a failing assert's
# unwind back into the harness.
kai_test_run_one                  pinned
kai_test_unwind                   pinned
kaix_test_drive                   pinned
kaix_bench_run_one                pinned
kaix_check_run_one                pinned
kai_check_record_param            pinned

# The process entry runs on thread 0's own stack, which no switch moves.
main                              pinned
